findings.scenario:
Decision §1.3 drops Active RO98 rows from the rivals because they "read '<current ICB> - code'". That premise is false for 58 Active RO98 rows in nhs-ods.tsv:
- the NHS England commissioning hubs: 13Q-14T, 15L, 27T, 32T, 76A, 85J, 97T, and 13X "CUMBRIA AND NORTH EAST COMMISSIONING HUB";
- the legacy Active CCGs 00W, 12A and 13P;
- about 32 London "<borough> - SUB-ICB REPORTING ENTITY" rows.

The guard at :352 (`child && rival.role == Role::SubIcb && rival.active`) drops all of them. I checked it with a Rust probe test (since deleted):
- `check("QHM", "Cumbria and North East Commissioning Hub")` returns `Better{13X}`;
- `child_check("QHM", "Cumbria and North East Commissioning Hub")` returns `Admitted`.

I then ran an end-to-end probe through `Db::plan_nhs_bodies` with the production adapters (app integration test, since deleted). Setup: QHM's PPON org 5; a provisional cluster 9 named for the hub, bound by a `GB-NHS-00P` party; an identity org 10 (`GBNHS16C`) with the same name. Result: both 9 and 10 are listed under QHM as `child` (zero-only / identity) with `denied: []` and in `eligible`. A cohort {5, 9, 10} reviewed over {5, 9, 10} reads `admitted`.

For now only `child-fold-unbuilt` stops the fold. The bigger risk is U4: §3.5 makes this same fn the bind gate. Any later mention named after the hub that carries 13T, 16C, 01H, 84H or 99C (QHM is phase A with a PPON survivor) would then bind silently to 31556600, NENC's survivor. S4's `identity` halt cannot catch it, because the name passes child_check. This is the NHS England class C that 523 owns, filed as NENC.

The sweep against the Python port found one other newly admitted name: 13P's Birmingham CrossCity CCG under QHL. That one is arguably BSol's lineage.
